{"module":"reconstruction","voice":"female","duration":212.2,"segments":[{"el":"title","text":"Reconstruction overthrown","start":0.0,"end":2.07,"words":[[0.05,1.17],[1.17,1.95]]},{"el":"intro","text":"For about a decade after the Civil War, the United States tried to become a multiracial democracy.","start":2.87,"end":8.83,"words":[[2.87,3.0],[3.0,3.33],[3.33,3.46],[3.46,3.85],[3.85,4.12],[4.12,4.25],[4.25,4.58],[4.58,4.77],[5.0,5.13],[5.13,5.66],[5.66,6.05],[6.05,6.38],[6.38,6.51],[6.51,6.84],[6.84,6.97],[6.97,7.7],[7.7,8.35]]},{"el":"intro","text":"How that effort was destroyed explains much of the century that followed.","start":9.18,"end":12.99,"words":[[9.19,9.38],[9.38,9.56],[9.56,9.8],[9.8,9.99],[9.99,10.47],[10.47,11.02],[11.02,11.26],[11.26,11.38],[11.38,11.51],[11.51,11.99],[11.99,12.18],[12.18,12.6]]},{"el":"h0","text":"A second founding","start":13.34,"end":14.64,"words":[[13.39,13.55],[13.55,14.02],[14.02,14.57]]},{"el":"s0","text":"The 13th Amendment (1865) ended slavery, the 14th (1868) made everyone born here a citizen with “equal protection of the laws,” and the 15th (1870) barred race discrimination in voting.","start":15.34,"end":32.97,"words":[[15.39,15.57],[15.57,16.12],[16.12,16.93],[16.93,18.29],[18.6,19.05],[19.05,19.87],[20.18,20.37],[20.37,20.91],[20.91,22.26],[22.58,22.94],[22.94,23.58],[23.58,23.85],[23.85,24.12],[24.12,24.3],[24.3,24.93],[24.93,25.2],[25.2,25.65],[25.65,26.47],[26.47,26.65],[26.65,26.83],[26.83,27.19],[27.19,27.46],[27.46,27.64],[27.64,28.28],[28.28,29.36],[29.68,29.95],[29.95,30.31],[30.31,31.58],[31.58,31.76],[31.76,32.39]]},{"el":"s0","text":"The Freedmen's Bureau helped found schools across the South.","start":33.32,"end":36.86,"words":[[33.38,33.51],[33.51,34.06],[34.06,34.54],[34.54,34.88],[34.88,35.22],[35.22,35.56],[35.56,35.9],[35.9,36.04],[36.04,36.38]]},{"el":"s0","text":"About 2,000 Black men held office, including 16 members of Congress such as Senators Hiram Revels and Blanche K. Bruce and Representative Robert Smalls.","start":37.21,"end":48.06,"words":[[37.26,37.64],[37.64,38.32],[38.32,38.62],[38.62,38.85],[38.85,39.15],[39.15,39.53],[39.79,40.4],[40.4,40.93],[40.93,41.38],[41.38,41.53],[41.53,42.06],[42.06,42.36],[42.36,42.52],[42.52,43.05],[43.05,43.5],[43.5,43.95],[43.95,44.18],[44.18,44.56],[44.56,44.86],[45.24,45.54],[45.54,45.77],[45.77,46.75],[46.75,47.13],[47.13,47.58]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Reconstruction legislatures created the South's first public school systems.","start":48.41,"end":53.27,"words":[[48.46,49.36],[49.36,50.18],[50.18,50.8],[50.8,50.94],[50.94,51.28],[51.28,51.56],[51.56,51.97],[51.97,52.25],[52.25,52.8]]},{"el":"h1","text":"Terror","start":54.17,"end":55.55,"words":[[54.19,55.35]]},{"el":"s1","text":"The Ku Klux Klan formed in 1865–66.","start":56.25,"end":60.36,"words":[[56.31,56.46],[56.46,56.61],[56.61,56.98],[56.98,57.28],[57.28,57.57],[57.57,57.72],[57.72,59.81]]},{"el":"s1","text":"White mobs killed dozens of Black people in Memphis and New Orleans in 1866, and as many as 150 at Colfax in 1873.","start":60.71,"end":71.18,"words":[[60.77,61.08],[61.08,61.4],[61.4,61.71],[61.71,62.19],[62.19,62.35],[62.35,62.66],[62.66,63.06],[63.06,63.22],[63.22,63.69],[63.69,63.93],[63.93,64.16],[64.16,64.64],[64.64,64.8],[64.8,66.06],[66.34,66.57],[66.57,66.73],[66.73,67.05],[67.05,67.2],[67.2,68.55],[68.55,68.7],[68.7,69.26],[69.26,69.42],[69.42,70.68]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Congress passed the Enforcement Acts and the Ku Klux Klan Act (1870–71), and President Grant sent troops and suspended habeas corpus in nine South Carolina counties.","start":71.53,"end":83.93,"words":[[71.53,72.08],[72.08,72.39],[72.39,72.55],[72.55,73.26],[73.26,73.57],[73.57,73.81],[73.81,73.96],[73.96,74.12],[74.12,74.51],[74.51,74.83],[74.83,75.06],[75.06,77.03],[77.3,77.54],[77.54,78.24],[78.24,78.64],[78.64,78.95],[78.95,79.34],[79.34,79.58],[79.58,80.29],[80.29,80.84],[80.84,81.23],[81.23,81.39],[81.39,81.7],[81.7,82.01],[82.01,82.72],[82.72,83.35]]},{"el":"s1","text":"For a time, it worked.","start":84.28,"end":85.88,"words":[[84.28,84.4],[84.4,84.52],[84.52,84.83],[85.04,85.16],[85.16,85.46]]},{"el":"h2","text":"The courts and Congress walk away","start":86.78,"end":89.0,"words":[[86.83,87.0],[87.0,87.34],[87.34,87.59],[87.59,88.18],[88.18,88.44],[88.44,88.78]]},{"el":"s2","text":"The Supreme Court narrowed the 14th Amendment in the Slaughter-House Cases (1873), freed the Colfax killers in U.S. v. Cruikshank (1876), and struck down the Civil Rights Act of 1875 in the Civil Rights Cases (1883).","start":89.7,"end":107.07,"words":[[89.75,89.9],[89.9,90.37],[90.37,90.6],[90.6,91.06],[91.06,91.21],[91.21,91.68],[91.68,92.37],[92.37,92.53],[92.53,92.68],[92.68,93.38],[93.38,93.84],[93.84,95.07],[95.34,95.65],[95.65,95.81],[95.81,96.35],[96.35,96.73],[96.73,96.89],[96.89,97.27],[97.66,97.89],[98.28,98.97],[98.97,100.28],[100.55,100.79],[100.79,101.17],[101.17,101.48],[101.48,101.63],[101.63,102.02],[102.02,102.41],[102.41,102.64],[102.64,102.79],[102.79,104.03],[104.03,104.18],[104.18,104.34],[104.34,104.72],[104.72,105.11],[105.11,105.57],[105.57,106.57]]},{"el":"s2","text":"In Plessy v. Ferguson (1896) it approved “separate but equal.”","start":107.42,"end":112.75,"words":[[107.48,107.63],[107.63,108.0],[108.0,108.22],[108.6,109.12],[109.12,110.24],[110.5,110.65],[110.65,111.1],[111.1,111.55],[111.55,111.77],[111.77,112.22]]},{"el":"h3","text":"The “Compromise of 1877”","start":113.65,"end":116.36,"words":[[113.7,113.86],[113.86,114.67],[114.67,114.84],[114.84,116.22]]},{"el":"s3","text":"After the disputed 1876 election, Rutherford B. Hayes became president and federal troops stopped protecting Reconstruction governments in the South.","start":117.06,"end":127.24,"words":[[117.12,117.4],[117.4,117.54],[117.54,118.16],[118.16,119.27],[119.27,119.83],[120.07,120.56],[120.56,120.77],[121.11,121.39],[121.39,121.81],[121.81,122.43],[122.43,122.64],[122.64,123.12],[123.12,123.47],[123.47,123.82],[123.82,124.44],[124.44,125.35],[125.35,126.04],[126.04,126.18],[126.18,126.32],[126.32,126.66]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Historians debate whether a formal deal was struck or the outcome reflected a broader Northern retreat.","start":127.59,"end":133.41,"words":[[127.64,128.35],[128.35,128.78],[128.78,129.06],[129.06,129.21],[129.21,129.56],[129.56,129.77],[129.77,129.99],[129.99,130.34],[130.34,130.41],[130.41,130.55],[130.55,130.98],[130.98,131.62],[131.62,131.76],[131.76,132.12],[132.12,132.47],[132.47,132.97]]},{"el":"s3","text":"The result is not debated: Southern states were left to white “Redeemer” governments.","start":133.76,"end":138.2,"words":[[133.8,133.92],[133.92,134.28],[134.28,134.4],[134.4,134.58],[134.58,135.12],[135.33,135.63],[135.63,135.99],[135.99,136.11],[136.11,136.35],[136.35,136.47],[136.47,136.71],[136.71,137.07],[137.07,137.73]]},{"el":"h4","text":"Jim Crow and lynching","start":139.1,"end":140.84,"words":[[139.12,139.47],[139.47,139.83],[139.83,140.1],[140.1,140.72]]},{"el":"s4","text":"States passed segregation laws and stripped Black voting rights (see the module on the vote).","start":141.54,"end":147.18,"words":[[141.57,142.02],[142.02,142.33],[142.33,143.17],[143.17,143.39],[143.39,143.62],[143.62,144.08],[144.08,144.38],[144.38,144.84],[144.84,145.22],[145.22,145.37],[145.37,145.52],[145.52,145.98],[145.98,146.13],[146.13,146.28],[146.28,146.67]]},{"el":"s4","text":"EJI documented more than 4,400 racial terror lynchings from 1877 to 1950.","start":147.53,"end":155.36,"words":[[147.58,147.9],[147.9,148.76],[148.76,148.92],[148.92,149.16],[149.16,150.57],[150.57,151.05],[151.05,151.36],[151.36,151.99],[151.99,152.31],[152.31,153.65],[153.65,153.8],[153.8,154.83]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Ida B. Wells exposed the lie that lynching punished crime in Southern Horrors (1892).","start":155.71,"end":161.82,"words":[[155.76,156.05],[156.05,156.26],[156.63,156.92],[156.92,157.5],[157.5,157.64],[157.64,157.86],[157.86,158.08],[158.08,158.59],[158.59,159.03],[159.03,159.39],[159.39,159.53],[159.53,159.9],[159.9,160.26],[160.26,161.28]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Congress failed to pass an anti-lynching law for more than a century, until the Emmett Till Antilynching Act was signed in 2022.","start":162.17,"end":170.37,"words":[[162.17,162.68],[162.68,163.04],[163.04,163.19],[163.19,163.4],[163.4,163.55],[163.55,164.35],[164.35,164.49],[164.49,164.64],[164.64,164.78],[164.78,165.0],[165.0,165.14],[165.14,165.79],[166.05,166.41],[166.41,166.55],[166.55,166.84],[166.84,167.06],[167.06,167.86],[167.86,168.07],[168.07,168.29],[168.29,168.65],[168.65,168.8],[168.8,169.88]]},{"el":"s4","text":"About 6 million Black Southerners left in the Great Migration (1910–1970).","start":170.72,"end":177.33,"words":[[170.77,171.17],[171.17,171.48],[171.48,171.96],[171.96,172.28],[172.28,172.83],[172.83,173.15],[173.15,173.31],[173.31,173.47],[173.47,173.86],[173.86,174.66],[174.66,176.8]]},{"el":"h5","text":"The “Lost Cause”","start":178.23,"end":179.53,"words":[[178.28,178.51],[178.51,178.97],[178.97,179.31]]},{"el":"s5","text":"The claim that the Civil War was about states' rights or tariffs rather than slavery spread after the war through textbooks, monuments and films.","start":180.23,"end":188.76,"words":[[180.28,180.42],[180.42,180.79],[180.79,181.01],[181.01,181.16],[181.16,181.53],[181.53,181.67],[181.67,181.89],[181.89,182.26],[182.26,182.7],[182.7,183.07],[183.07,183.14],[183.14,183.58],[183.58,183.88],[183.88,184.1],[184.1,184.69],[184.69,185.05],[185.05,185.35],[185.35,185.49],[185.49,185.64],[185.64,185.86],[185.86,186.6],[186.85,187.59],[187.59,187.81],[187.81,188.25]]},{"el":"s5","text":"The secession documents say otherwise.","start":189.11,"end":191.33,"words":[[189.15,189.27],[189.27,189.68],[189.68,190.26],[190.26,190.44],[190.44,190.9]]},{"el":"s5","text":"Mississippi's declared: “Our position is thoroughly identified with the institution of slavery.”","start":191.68,"end":197.49,"words":[[191.73,192.35],[192.35,192.91],[193.15,193.36],[193.36,193.84],[193.84,193.98],[193.98,194.4],[194.4,195.16],[195.16,195.37],[195.37,195.51],[195.51,196.27],[196.27,196.41],[196.41,197.04]]},{"el":"s5","text":"Most Confederate monuments were put up between about 1900 and the 1920s, and again in the 1950s and 1960s, during Jim Crow and the civil rights movement, not right after the war.","start":197.84,"end":211.31,"words":[[197.89,198.25],[198.25,198.95],[198.95,199.65],[199.65,199.79],[199.79,200.0],[200.0,200.14],[200.14,200.56],[200.56,200.91],[200.91,201.89],[201.89,202.11],[202.11,202.25],[202.25,203.3],[203.54,203.75],[203.75,204.04],[204.04,204.18],[204.18,204.32],[204.32,205.23],[205.23,205.44],[205.44,206.49],[206.74,207.23],[207.23,207.51],[207.51,207.79],[207.79,208.0],[208.0,208.14],[208.14,208.49],[208.49,208.84],[208.84,209.4],[209.65,209.86],[209.86,210.14],[210.14,210.42],[210.42,210.56],[210.56,210.77]]}]}